+# Apple III profile
+
+`iigs_disk` also handles WOZ-capable Apple-III cores (S0 advertises WOZ).
+Older Apple-III builds keep their existing generic image path.
+
+- S0: internal Disk III (.D1).
+- S1: first external Disk III (.D2).
+- S2/S3: raw block-device payloads, read-only. The current FPGA exposes only
+  S0/S1; these assignments reserve the block-device interface without treating
+  S1 as the Apple //e hard-disk slot.
+
+The shared 5.25 encoder uses proper 10-bit sync words. Apple III's profile adds
+its standard synchronized track rotation to sector sources, and writes 51,424
+cells per track, read at 3.875 us (INFO timing 31), rather than the bare 50,304
+at 4 us. The drive model consumes one cell per bit the machine writes, 3.91 us
+each, and Apple's Disk /// formatter judges drive speed by how many sync nibbles
+fit between sectors: 19 to 24 is acceptable and 22 is a correctly adjusted
+drive. It starts at 27 and shrinks until sixteen sectors of 10n + 2,988 cells
+close the track. On 50,304 cells it reported "drive is too fast" and the
+Confidence Program's Align check failed; on 51,904 it closed at 25 and reported
+"too slow". 51,424 closes at 22. Native WOZ is
+validated and served unchanged; NIB preserves its address fields and data bytes.
+
+SOS copy protection (BFM.INIT2) reads one address-field volume byte on each of
+tracks 9–16. When those tracks are synchronized and the values differ, SOS uses
+them as a key and decrypts SOS.INTERP in memory. Sector images carry no address
+fields, so the key is rebuilt only when it is needed: Main finds SOS.INTERP on
+the volume, decodes a sample with the key, and keeps whichever version has the
+byte statistics of 6502 code. A sector dump of a protected original (encrypted
+interpreter) gets the key; a deprotected or never-protected disk does not,
+because SOS would otherwise "decrypt" working code and stop with SYSTEM FAILURE
+$06. 140K ProDOS-order images use the standard sector map (block 2 is DOS
+sectors 11 and 10).
+
+Native WOZ2 bitstream files permit writes within existing track allocations.
+Main preserves metadata and unrelated chunks, clears CRC to zero before the
+first write, and rejects writes outside those allocations. WOZ1, FLUX, archives
+and protected images remain read-only, as do block payloads.
+
+DSK, DO, PO and sector-order 2MG sources are writable in place. The drive saves
+a track one 512-byte block at a time, so the track is torn until its last block
+arrives. Main decodes it then, with a strict parser: address checksum and track
+number, a data field within the gap that follows, valid GCR codes, data checksum
+and both epilogs. Sectors that verify replace the source's, in the source's own
+sector order and behind any 2MG header, with a single 4 KiB write per track; the
+image is opened O_SYNC, and sixteen sector writes per track held the drive's
+cache busy long enough to break SOS's formatter. A damaged sector is never
+stored, and Main reports how many were not saved. A read request from the drive
+also stores a track whose save was cut short. NIB and DC42 sources
+stay read-only. The //e and IIgs keep their existing whole-track write-back.
+
+The hardware still implements Apple III's P6 sequencer, independent spindle/I/O
+selection, motor timeout, disk-change latch and physical bit timing. Main handles
+file formats and file I/O via the existing SD protocol, including multi-block
+transfers up to 16 KiB. No new FPGA/Main transport protocol is introduced.
+
+`tests/apple3/run.sh` exercises the shared backend, including //e/IIgs
+regressions. The Apple III core's simulator exercises the real-time disk path
+using the same converted WOZ output.
